comment_data.body:
> In languages with templates (Rust, C++), the same functionality would be very useful whenever removing a template e.g. doing `sdf` on `Vec<SomeType>` should just keep `SomeType`.
> 
> Is there a way to customize `sdf` for that, or maybe introduce another shortcut?

Of course. You can pretty much reuse [how `f` itself is defined](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/64a23d04cce6d600f18d5d1610d319f6efa08958/lua/mini/surround.lua#L1130-L1137). Here is an example of how to do it for `t` surrounding (will override "tag" surrounding). Here is how to do it globally:

```lua
require('mini.surround').setup({
  custom_surroundings = {
    t = {
      input = { '%f[%w_%.][%w_%.]+%b<>', '^.-<().*()>$' },
      output = function()
        local fun_name = MiniSurround.user_input('Template name')
        if fun_name == nil then return nil end
        return { left = fun_name .. '<', right = '>' }
      end,
    },
  },
})
```

A more reasonable approach would be to only redefine this surrounding for filetypes that you want. For example:
- Create '~/.config/nvim/after/ftplugin/rust.lua' file (if not already).
- Add creation of the following [buffer-local config](https://nvim-mini.org/mini.nvim/doc/mini-surround.html#module-setup):

    ```lua
    vim.b.minisurround_config = {
      custom_surroundings = {
        t = {
          input = { '%f[%w_%.][%w_%.]+%b<>', '^.-<().*()>$' },
          output = function()
            local fun_name = MiniSurround.user_input('Template name')
            if fun_name == nil then return nil end
            return { left = fun_name .. '<', right = '>' }
          end,
        },
      },
    }
    ```

> Also slightly orthogonal but similar. Often function calls have a path in them e.g. `module::function(args)`, but `sdf` does not work on it. Is it possible to customize it to remove `module::function(...)`?

By default `f` recognizes function name as the string that [contains only alphanumeric+`_`+`.` characters](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/5121416626b82bb2d2198f3ee40261a84a4813f5/doc/mini-surround.txt#L23-L24). To adjust that (globally) follow the same approach as above, but customize only `input` part of `f` surrounding:

```lua
require('mini.surround').setup({
  custom_surroundings = {
    f = { input = { '%f[%w_%.:][%w_%.:]+%b()', '^.-%(().*()%)$' } },
  },
})
```

